<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<topic id="Sending-Messages-With-SMTP" revisionNumber="1">
  <developerConceptualDocument
    xmlns="http://ddue.schemas.microsoft.com/authoring/2003/5"
    xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">

    <introduction>
      <para>
        <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpClient</codeEntityReference> is used to send messages
        created with MimeKit. This topic covers sending messages, controlling the envelope, handling
        rejected recipients, requesting delivery status notifications and other SMTP features.
      </para>
      <para>
        For information about connecting securely, logging and error handling, see
        <link xlink:href="Connecting-To-A-Server"/>. For information about authenticating, see
        <link xlink:href="Authentication"/>.
      </para>
      <autoOutline />
    </introduction>

    <section address="SendingMessages">
      <title>Sending a message</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          The typical workflow is to connect, authenticate, send one or more messages and then disconnect:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\SmtpExamples.cs" region="SendMessage"/>
        <para>
          Connecting and authenticating is relatively expensive, so if you have multiple messages to send,
          reuse the same connection for all of them rather than connecting once per message:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\SmtpExamples.cs" region="SendMessages"/>
        <alert class="note">
          <para>
            <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpClient</codeEntityReference> is not thread-safe. To send
            messages concurrently, use one <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpClient</codeEntityReference>
            per thread (or a pool of clients).
          </para>
        </alert>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="Envelope">
      <title>Senders and recipients</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          Unless you specify otherwise, the SMTP envelope is derived from the message headers:
        </para>
        <list class="bullet">
          <listItem>
            <para>
              The envelope sender (<literal>MAIL FROM</literal>) is the <literal>Resent-Sender</literal> or
              first <literal>Resent-From</literal> mailbox if the message has been resent, otherwise the
              <literal>Sender</literal> or first <literal>From</literal> mailbox.
            </para>
          </listItem>
          <listItem>
            <para>
              The envelope recipients (<literal>RCPT TO</literal>) are the mailboxes in the
              <literal>Resent-To</literal>, <literal>Resent-Cc</literal> and <literal>Resent-Bcc</literal> headers
              if the message has been resent, otherwise the mailboxes in the <literal>To</literal>,
              <literal>Cc</literal> and <literal>Bcc</literal> headers.
            </para>
          </listItem>
        </list>
        <para>
          The <literal>Bcc</literal> and <literal>Resent-Bcc</literal> headers are never transmitted, so
          the other recipients cannot see them.
        </para>
        <para>
          To use a different envelope (for example, to send a message to a different list of recipients
          than the ones listed in the headers), pass the sender and recipients explicitly using a
          <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.SendRequest</codeEntityReference> or
          <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpSendRequest</codeEntityReference>:
        </para>
        <code language="c#">
var request = new SmtpSendRequest (message, sender, recipients);

client.Send (request);
        </code>
        <para>
          If the server supports the <literal>SMTPUTF8</literal> extension, you can send messages that
          contain international (non-ASCII) email addresses and headers by enabling
          <codeEntityReference>P:MimeKit.FormatOptions.International</codeEntityReference>:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\SmtpExamples.cs" region="SendMessageWithOptions"/>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="SendResults">
      <title>Examining the result</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          <codeEntityReference autoUpgrade="true">Overload:MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpClient.Send</codeEntityReference>
          returns a <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.SendResult</codeEntityReference> (an
          <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpSendResult</codeEntityReference> when using
          <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpClient</codeEntityReference>) containing the server's
          final response along with the lists of accepted and rejected recipients. Many servers include
          a queue identifier in the response text, which can be useful for tracking the message in the
          server's logs.
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\SmtpSendResultExamples.cs" region="SendResult"/>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="RejectedRecipients">
      <title>Handling rejected recipients</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          By default, if the server rejects any of the recipients, an
          <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpCommandException</codeEntityReference> is thrown with an
          <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpCommandException.ErrorCode</codeEntityReference> of
          <codeInline>RecipientNotAccepted</codeInline> and the message is not sent to anyone. The
          <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpCommandException.Mailbox</codeEntityReference> property
          identifies the rejected recipient.
        </para>
        <para>
          To deliver the message to the remaining recipients instead, subclass
          <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpSendRequest</codeEntityReference> and override
          <codeEntityReference>M:MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpSendRequest.OnRecipientNotAccepted(MimeKit.MailboxAddress,MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpResponse)</codeEntityReference>
          so that it does not throw. Rejected recipients are then listed in
          <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.SendResult.RejectedRecipients</codeEntityReference>.
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\SmtpSendResultExamples.cs" region="LenientSendRequest"/>
        <para>
          <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpSendRequest</codeEntityReference> also has
          <codeEntityReference>M:MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpSendRequest.OnSenderAccepted(MimeKit.MailboxAddress,MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpResponse)</codeEntityReference>,
          <codeEntityReference>M:MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpSendRequest.OnSenderNotAccepted(MimeKit.MailboxAddress,MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpResponse)</codeEntityReference>,
          <codeEntityReference>M:MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpSendRequest.OnRecipientAccepted(MimeKit.MailboxAddress,MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpResponse)</codeEntityReference> and
          <codeEntityReference>M:MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpSendRequest.OnNoRecipientsAccepted</codeEntityReference>
          methods that can be overridden to observe each step of the transaction.
        </para>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="DeliveryStatusNotifications">
      <title>Delivery status notifications</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          If the server supports the <literal>DSN</literal> extension, you can ask it to notify the sender
          when a message is delivered, delayed or fails to be delivered to each recipient. Set the
          <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpSendRequest.DeliveryStatusNotifications</codeEntityReference>
          property (or override
          <codeEntityReference>M:MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpSendRequest.GetDeliveryStatusNotifications(MimeKit.MailboxAddress)</codeEntityReference>
          to choose per recipient) and optionally an
          <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpSendRequest.EnvelopeId</codeEntityReference> that will be
          included in the notifications so that you can correlate them with the original message:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\SmtpExamples.cs" region="DeliveryStatusNotification"/>
        <para>
          Delivery status notifications arrive as ordinary messages in the sender's mailbox. See
          <codeEntityReference>T:MimeKit.MessageDeliveryStatus</codeEntityReference> for parsing them:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\MessageDeliveryStatusExamples.cs" region="ProcessDeliveryStatusNotification"/>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="Capabilities">
      <title>Server capabilities</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          After connecting, the <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpClient.Capabilities</codeEntityReference>
          property lists the SMTP extensions supported by the server. MailKit automatically takes advantage
          of extensions such as <literal>PIPELINING</literal>, <literal>8BITMIME</literal>,
          <literal>BINARYMIME</literal> and <literal>CHUNKING</literal> when they are available.
          <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpClient.MaxSize</codeEntityReference> reports the
          maximum message size accepted by the server, if advertised.
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\SmtpExamples.cs" region="Capabilities"/>
        <para>
          Some servers also support the <literal>VRFY</literal> and <literal>EXPN</literal> commands, which can
          be used to verify an address or expand a mailing list. Most public servers disable these commands
          to prevent address harvesting.
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\SmtpExamples.cs" region="VerifyAddress"/>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\SmtpExamples.cs" region="ExpandAlias"/>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="PickupDirectory">
      <title>Saving messages to a pickup directory</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          Some servers (such as IIS) pick up messages from a directory on disk rather than accepting them
          over the network. Messages written to a pickup directory must be "byte-stuffed" in the same way
          that they would be when sent using the <literal>DATA</literal> command. The
          <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpDataFilter</codeEntityReference> can be used to do this:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\SmtpExamples.cs" region="SaveToPickupDirectory"/>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\SmtpExamples.cs" region="LoadFromPickupDirectory"/>
      </content>
    </section>

